  "text_md": "1960 ROBERT LAUTER\n\nIj1)I\n\n. Similar, to µ2 := (λ1,λ2) ∈ QB(Z), there exists j2 ∈ {2,... ,  + 2} with µ2 ∈ ( QB(Z)\n\nj1 ∈ {2,... ,  + 2} with µ1 ∈ ( QB(Z)\n\nj1−1\n\nIj2)I\n\n. Suppose j2 < j1. Since QB(Z)\n\nj2−1\n\nIj2 is open, by (2.5) there exist open sets Vk ⊆ RH\n\nk\n\nIj2 ⊆ QB(Z)\n\nIj1−1\n\nwhich contradicts our choice of j1.\n\nwith λk ∈ Vk, k = 1,2, such that V1×V2 V1 V2 ⊆ QB(Z)\n\nIj1)I\n\n. By Lemma 3.4 the space ( QB(Z)\n\nNow suppose j1 = j2, i.e. µ1,µ2 ∈ ( QB(Z)\n\nj1−1\n\nIj1)I\n\nis Hausdorﬀ in its relative topology, which is impossible because we have µ1 ∈ W2 ∩ W1 for any choice of open sets Wk ⊆ QB(Z) with µk ∈ Wk. Consequently, we have j1 < j2, and iterating this procedure leads to a sequence 2 ≤ j1 < ··· < jk\n\nj1−1\n\n0 ≤ + 2 which gives ≥ k0 − 1, and completes the proof in the case k0 = n. On the other hand, if k0 < n note that by Lemma 3.4 we have\n\nψ\n\nk0\n\nk=1\n\nIjk\n\nQB(Z)\n\nk0\n\nIjk−1 =\n\nk=1\n\n+1\n\nk−1 ⊆\n\nTj\n\nTj\n\nj=1\n\ncorresponds to a µk, i.e. to an inﬁnite-dimensional irreducible representation. Since QB(Z) also has one-dimensional irreducible representations by Theorem 2.1 and (2.4), we have k0 + 1 ≤ + 1, i.e. the length of QB(Z) is at least k0.\n\nwhere each Tj\n\nk\n\n![](<958d5687f44a6c99cf15cbc802731abf937303fd_page_6_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<958d5687f44a6c99cf15cbc802731abf937303fd_page_6_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n![](<958d5687f44a6c99cf15cbc802731abf937303fd_page_6_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\n![](<958d5687f44a6c99cf15cbc802731abf937303fd_page_6_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\nOf course, a combination of Lemma 3.1, Lemma 3.2, and Lemma 3.5 completes the proof of Theorem 1.1.\n\nIt remains to prove Proposition 1.2. Recall that the composition series (1.1) is called stratiﬁed if for any irreducible representation π of B with π(Jk) = {0} we have π(Jk+1) = π(Jk) [13, Deﬁnition 0.3]. Moreover, a solving, stratiﬁed composition series coincides with the maximal radical series by [13, Proposition 0.4]. If we abbreviate := lengthQB(Z), then the series\n\n(3.1) QB(Z) ⊇ J +1 ⊇ J ⊇ ··· ⊇ J1 = {0}\n\nwith Jk given by (2.3) is solving of minimal length by Theorem 1.1 and the proof of Lemma 3.2. Using the description (2.4) of the irreducible representations of QB(Z) and the compatibility conditions (2.1) and (2.2), it is straightforward to check that the series (3.1) is stratiﬁed. Thus, it is the maximal radical series for QB(Z), hence, an application of Lemma 3.1 completes the proof of Proposition 1.2.\n\nAcknowledgment\n\nThe author would like to thank Professor B. Gramsch for drawing the author’s attention to the b-calculus, Professor B. A. Plamenevskij for suggesting the problem of describing the Jacobson topology of the algebra B(Z,bΩ12), and Professor A. Yu. Kokotov for raising the question what the length of B(Z,bΩ12) is at the Spring School Operator algebras with symbolic structure on singular manifolds, index theory, and asymptotics at Potsdam (February, 98).\n\n![](<958d5687f44a6c99cf15cbc802731abf937303fd_page_6_images/imageFile5.png>)\n\n![](<958d5687f44a6c99cf15cbc802731abf937303fd_page_6_images/imageFile6.png>)\n\nFinally, the author wishes to thank Professor R. B. Melrose and the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where this note was written, for the invitation and hospitality.\n\nLicense or copyright restrictions may apply to redistribution; see https://www.ams.org/journal-terms-of-use\n\n",
