      "text": "It remains to prove Proposition 1.2. Recall that the composition series (1.1) is called stratiﬁed if for any irreducible representation π of B with π(Jk) = {0} we have π(Jk+1) = π(Jk) [13, Deﬁnition 0.3]. Moreover, a solving, stratiﬁed composition series coincides with the maximal radical series by [13, Proposition 0.4]. If we abbreviate := lengthQB(Z), then the series",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.20704575163398695,
        "y0": 0.6009166666666667,
        "x1": 0.6346127450980392,
        "y1": 0.6251818181818182
      },
      "text": "(3.1) QB(Z) ⊇ J +1 ⊇ J ⊇ ··· ⊇ J1 = {0}",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.20703758169934638,
        "y0": 0.6260681818181818,
        "x1": 0.793328431372549,
        "y1": 0.7018573232323233
      },
      "text": "with Jk given by (2.3) is solving of minimal length by Theorem 1.1 and the proof of Lemma 3.2. Using the description (2.4) of the irreducible representations of QB(Z) and the compatibility conditions (2.1) and (2.2), it is straightforward to check that the series (3.1) is stratiﬁed. Thus, it is the maximal radical series for QB(Z), hence, an application of Lemma 3.1 completes the proof of Proposition 1.2.",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.4274395424836602,
        "y0": 0.7264002525252525,
        "x1": 0.5727630718954249,
        "y1": 0.738979797979798
      },
      "text": "Acknowledgment",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.2070392156862745,
        "y0": 0.7463724747474747,
        "x1": 0.7933676470588236,
        "y1": 0.8391313131313132
      },
      "text": "The author would like to thank Professor B. Gramsch for drawing the author’s attention to the b-calculus, Professor B. A. Plamenevskij for suggesting the problem of describing the Jacobson topology of the algebra B(Z,bΩ12), and Professor A. Yu. Kokotov for raising the question what the length of B(Z,bΩ12) is at the Spring School Operator algebras with symbolic structure on singular manifolds, index theory, and asymptotics at Potsdam (February, 98).",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
